      <data key="d11">1748426852</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member State" target="Commission">
      <data key="d6">72.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States are required to communicate with the Commission regarding justified or unjustified measures related to AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The Commission plays a pivotal role in overseeing the compliance of Member States with established EU regulations, particularly in the context of artificial intelligence (AI) systems. Member States are required to report to the Commission whenever they identify that specific standards may not meet the required benchmarks. This collaboration extends to the implementation of EU regulations, where Member States work alongside the Commission to address any non-compliance issues that arise.

Moreover, Member States have an obligation to notify the Commission of any national laws adopted under relevant regulations, reinforcing a structure of oversight and compliance. They must also inform the Commission about the risks associated with AI systems and the actions taken to mitigate such risks. In turn, the Commission engages with Member States to evaluate the national measures in place regarding these AI system risks.

The communication between the Commission and Member States is crucial, especially concerning the competence and responsibilities of notified bodies. Additionally, the Commission actively monitors the authorizations issued by Member States to ensure that these are in accordance with Union law, specifically for high-risk AI systems. This comprehensive oversight framework highlights the cooperative relationship between the Commission and Member States in ensuring adherence to EU regulations and managing the complexities surrounding AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The Member State must inform the Commission regarding national measures taken about AI systems and compliance issues.</data>
      <data key="d8">collaboration,compliance,compliance reporting,cooperation,governance,government oversight,information sharing,intergovernmental communication,legislative framework,oversight,policy enforcement,regulation oversight,regulatory compliance,regulatory framework,regulatory oversight,risk management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-85726b1098cb96ad81df40234ad5da52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee3505c356c8e76e9f364115f4eb7ea3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae5c70e1b98b5f33eb047bb55022e9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9c53f2e5a51e855855cf3895a52eefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e43bf41139a924443fd8b200d694f24&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec59dcaa7ce835ce9dd1c42d507cf75f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37d8a464e00ff30e1ae415351b48c67e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93c18ff4b78baf6a152a71902a137a02</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/37/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/37/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/46/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/41/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/82/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/81/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748428504</data>
    </edge>
