    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Member States and Third Countries Agreements">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Agreements among Member States provide the framework for effective cooperation and law enforcement protocols with Third Countries.</data>
      <data key="d8">agreements,international relations</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3e7a853f20702bb64df032bcfe5c2e04</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/22/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426164</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="The Board">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Board facilitates coordination and shares expertise among the Member States to enforce AI regulations effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;The Board is composed of representatives from Member States, reflecting their interests in the AI eco-system and coordinating implementation tasks.</data>
      <data key="d8">cooperation,coordination,regulatory support,representation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a753b958cb652b7d0935c682d40c6af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-756bc5b856a1d06a78ffd0aeb1d318b4</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/66/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/149/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748427535</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="AI Regulation">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States are involved in the implementation and enforcement of AI Regulation, ensuring compliance within their jurisdictions.</data>
      <data key="d8">governance,implementation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a753b958cb652b7d0935c682d40c6af2</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/149/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426184</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="National Competent Authorities">
      <data key="d6">49.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States are required to allocate adequate resources and personnel to the National Competent Authorities to ensure the effective implementation of regulations. These Member States are responsible for establishing and designating the National Competent Authorities, which are tasked with overseeing regulatory compliance. The National Competent Authorities operate independently and are established to guarantee adherence to regulations, as well as to ensure the application and enforcement of various rules, including administrative fines. Additionally, these authorities facilitate the creation and oversight of AI regulatory sandboxes, further supporting innovation while maintaining regulatory standards. In summary, Member States play a crucial role in empowering National Competent Authorities to function effectively in enforcing regulations and overseeing compliance.</data>
      <data key="d8">authority delegation,compliance,enforcement,governance,organizational role,regulatory bodies,regulatory compliance,regulatory framework,regulatory oversight,regulatory structure,resource allocation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4fd25b8a323071b471e7df42fd1a91ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1bd36f2082e6f670210d08c02e3d502&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a9e7815086e5c37a3beb2705ee18d8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-977670aa5941763001334246314af150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24b5f2c129e84e53724de4a8e9f61091</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/153/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/70/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/99/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/138/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748428381</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Article 4(2) TEU">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Article 4(2) TEU defines the overarching responsibilities and authority of Member States regarding national security.</data>
      <data key="d8">legal framework,state responsibility</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9a65cc9d5292452e105480a128495e17</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/24/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426212</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="High-Risk AI Systems">
      <data key="d6">31.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Member States are mandated to identify and maintain a list of public authorities tasked with overseeing high-risk AI systems.".&lt;SEP&gt;Member States can create laws governing high-risk AI systems in accordance with Union law, impacting their implementation and oversight.&lt;SEP&gt;Member States must ensure that high-risk AI systems comply with the regulations set by notifying authorities and notified bodies.&lt;SEP&gt;Member States have the responsibility to ensure that high-risk AI systems that comply with regulations are not obstructed in the market.</data>
      <data key="d8">AI governance,enforcement,government responsibility,legislation,market access,regulatory compliance,regulatory oversight</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-69dd3a3cd7b3f9b54e8170860963df4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e442b04ad431e8e1a74bf9fa0f8bc5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32ccfe5fb33f83ead7b5c0eb2f0576d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5bcf2e7f28d217ab321153caf980fbfe</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/77/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/36/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/129/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748427797</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Post-Remote Biometric Identification Systems">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States have the authority to introduce more restrictive laws concerning the use of post-remote biometric identification systems, reinforcing regulatory frameworks.</data>
      <data key="d8">legislation,regulatory authority</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-32ccfe5fb33f83ead7b5c0eb2f0576d9</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426283</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="National Data Protection Authority">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States are responsible for appointing National Data Protection Authorities that oversee compliance with laws regarding personal data, including biometric identification systems.</data>
      <data key="d8">compliance oversight,data protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-32ccfe5fb33f83ead7b5c0eb2f0576d9</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/26/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426283</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Conformity Assessment Bodies">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States provide information about conformity assessment bodies they regulate to ensure compliance with EU standards.</data>
      <data key="d8">compliance assurance,regulatory oversight</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2b7923bde8b2532e6e5a248aa4cfb8a0</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/30/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426299</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Penalties">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States are required to implement penalties that align with the provisions of the Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d8">compliance,implementation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f567c4be37bce779f921de15ad5f2a7f</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/179/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426328</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Market Surveillance Authorities">
      <data key="d6">47.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Market Surveillance Authorities are designated entities within each Member State responsible for overseeing artificial intelligence (AI) systems and ensuring compliance with relevant reporting regulations. Each Member State is tasked with the responsibility of establishing its own Market Surveillance Authority to enforce compliance according to the regulations set forth by the European Commission. These authorities communicate their decisions regarding the testing compliance of AI systems to other Member States, thereby facilitating a coordinated approach within the EU framework. Additionally, Member States may appoint specific Market Surveillance Authorities to enforce various regulations pertaining to AI systems, ensuring that all operations align with EU law. Overall, Market Surveillance Authorities play a crucial role in the governance of AI systems throughout the Member States, striving to uphold regulatory standards and foster compliance.</data>
      <data key="d8">authority,enforcement,inter-agency communication,jurisdiction,national authority,national enforcement,regulatory collaboration,regulatory compliance,regulatory designation,regulatory framework,regulatory oversight</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f123082dcc9e4aa9993ce2a5fd0377de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88c5862ea0c154cbf48da804ec3ebf8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dda722630e3447daa9258a423b2cbedd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff5e73e3b7c1de9ffa48661e94e87a5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aed721d84482f8eb2ef2f8c288d59095&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9e2425d325b96342fb76768707fe3cf</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/73/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/88/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/79/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/76/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/74/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/156/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748427568</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="National Public Authorities">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States are required to identify and make publicly available lists of national public authorities enforcing Union law.</data>
      <data key="d8">governmental responsibility,transparency</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-69dd3a3cd7b3f9b54e8170860963df4f</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/77/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426491</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Union Law">
      <data key="d6">26.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States must comply with Union law, which includes directives such as Directive (EU) 2022/2557 that impacts national policies and practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Member States operate within the framework of Union Law, managing their competencies and responsibilities towards AI regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;Union Law applies to Member States and requires them to implement its stipulations regarding employment and working conditions.</data>
      <data key="d8">EU governance,compliance,legal compliance,legal framework,legal governance,member compliance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c89fbb177b0adfb819460dd999b9788d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-654b17e6ab435ca442e9075545af0c79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afaea4dcd95d49cc4d21cf9078216b27</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/2/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/9/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/33/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748427585</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="national security">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States have the authority to determine national security measures, including how AI systems are managed and regulated.</data>
      <data key="d8">security,sovereignty</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-217dd0b241eb263a726e72dd92814c0a</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/2/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426717</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="fundamental rights and freedoms">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States are responsible for ensuring that their laws, including those regarding AI, protect the fundamental rights and freedoms of individuals.</data>
      <data key="d8">human rights,regulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-217dd0b241eb263a726e72dd92814c0a</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/2/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426717</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Consumer Protection Laws">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States can implement their own consumer protection laws that align with or are more favorable than Union regulations.</data>
      <data key="d8">national laws,regulatory implementation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ac3e101757a5789343d428cd14e4874e</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/2/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426717</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Product Safety Regulations">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States are responsible for enforcing product safety regulations that comply with Union law, ensuring consumer protection.</data>
      <data key="d8">compliance,regulatory implementation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ac3e101757a5789343d428cd14e4874e</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/2/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426717</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Regulation 176">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Regulation 176 is designed to impose measures that Member States need to implement for protecting health and safety regarding AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d8">health and safety,implementation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1db48dde4f93da16c9531d8351b22c36</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/176/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426743</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Union AI Testing Support Structures">
      <data key="d6">15.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States will benefit from the Union AI Testing Support Structures for better enforcement of AI Systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Union AI Testing Support Structures are established to provide support for the Member States regarding the enforcement of AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d8">capability building,enforcement,implementation support,support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b9c4fb703fd804a96a166ec00e7efb74</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/152/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426745</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="AI Systems">
      <data key="d6">23.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States are exploring national rules to ensure the trustworthy and safe deployment of AI systems within their jurisdictions.&lt;SEP&gt;Member States are required to implement AI systems according to the regulations set by the European Parliament and Council, impacting asylum processing.&lt;SEP&gt;Member States are tasked with facilitating the drawing up of codes of conduct for the deployment of specific requirements related to AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d8">AI governance,compliance,governance,implementation,regulation,regulatory support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-30361d2283908d9b7babf63ae110a691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f13cd7281a4984edb2a5651f8501ee3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-073b0a5a910fcaf1208261b2d390896a</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/3/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/60/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/95/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748428577</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="National Rules">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States may establish National Rules to regulate the usage and deployment of AI technology in a manner that aligns with EU standards.</data>
      <data key="d8">governance,regulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-073b0a5a910fcaf1208261b2d390896a</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/3/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426747</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="Uniform Obligations">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Uniform Obligations are established to prevent fragmentation in regulations among Member States regarding the deployment of AI Systems.</data>
      <data key="d8">consistency,regulatory harmony</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-073b0a5a910fcaf1208261b2d390896a</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/3/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426747</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Member States" target="AI Office">
      <data key="d6">78.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Member States and the AI Office work together to address the regulatory and ethical aspects of AI deployment.&lt;SEP&gt;The AI Office collaborates with Member States to address the regulation and ethical aspects of AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The AI Office collaborates with Member States to encourage the development of codes of conduct for AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The AI Office coordinates actions mandated by Member States to support SMEs and Start-Ups regarding AI regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The AI Office is dedicated to assisting Member States in developing the technical expertise and regulatory frameworks necessary for the effective implementation of AI systems. To ensure robust AI governance, Member States are required to support and facilitate the tasks of the AI Office. They play a crucial role by providing input needed for the AI Office to effectively assess the regulatory framework concerning AI systems. Additionally, Member States can initiate requests for the AI Office to update guidelines in accordance with Union harmonisation law. The collaboration between the AI Office and the Member States extends to supporting investigations and compliance activities specifically pertaining to high-risk AI systems. Overall, the AI Office relies on the engagement and cooperation of the Member States to fulfill its responsibilities as outlined in the relevant regulations.</data>
      <data key="d8">collaboration,governance,implementation,organizational support,regulatory collaboration,regulatory development,regulatory framework,regulatory implementation,regulatory input,regulatory request,support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-70fac425f08c058417791cd1d66faab2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ede6b0da3acfca47d4e90d51eb5fc4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e28a697924414bd64214b20162364786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30361d2283908d9b7babf63ae110a691&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8363b7ff75e2a59a44c744564b484596&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7982e6435aa73ec0e21cfbbfe3f5ea87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02c1809d1e7a16e9c8b3d0effff3a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf6ae679199f6853488d22af6ebdf75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a7b16baf52f0573b141bf124a701c7a</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/112/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/96/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/148/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/66/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/95/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/74/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/62/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/64/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748428577</data>
    </edge>
