    </edge>
    <edge source="AI Systems" target="Annex III">
      <data key="d6">25.0</data>
      <data key="d7">AI systems classified in Annex III are deemed high-risk based on criteria set by the European Commission for health and rights safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Annex III lists conditions under which specific AI systems are deemed high-risk, influencing their market entry and use.&lt;SEP&gt;Annex III provides a detailed categorization of AI systems that the Commission considers when setting regulatory conditions.</data>
      <data key="d8">AI classification,compliance,market regulation,risk assessment,risk classification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1bca7fe15bcbe076f97f98b2f3479775&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40fea6c01126f1d5b9269b29322c34de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fe20aa0cce0b31f6a43c755317350d6</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/6/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/7/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748428151</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AI Systems" target="National Public Authorities">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">National Public Authorities are responsible for regulating AI Systems to ensure adherence to safety and rights protection standards.</data>
      <data key="d8">oversight,regulation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8484bd5b0709234bd04f595f21062cb4</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/157/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426074</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AI Systems" target="Fundamental Rights">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">AI Systems must respect and uphold Fundamental Rights as part of their operational framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulations concerning AI Systems are established to protect Fundamental Rights against potential risks posed by such technologies.</data>
      <data key="d8">ethical considerations,human rights,human rights protection,technology governance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8484bd5b0709234bd04f595f21062cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8189d1d23247190f250b589837018fa0</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/157/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/118/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748426770</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AI Systems" target="Natural Persons">
      <data key="d6">117.0</data>
      <data key="d7">AI Systems play a critical role in evaluating and classifying Natural Persons based on their social behavior, personality characteristics, and various other metrics, which can lead to social scoring. This process raises significant concerns regarding privacy, individual rights, and potential discriminatory outcomes, particularly when these systems are deployed in sensitive contexts like law enforcement for profiling purposes or in financial services for credit evaluations. 

As Natural Persons interact with AI Systems, it is imperative that these systems are designed with a strong emphasis on user safety, accuracy, and the protection of individual rights. Transparency in the interactions between AI Systems and Natural Persons is also essential, as it helps to ensure accountability in decision-making processes that can critically impact individuals' access to resources, services, and their overall freedoms. 

The implications of AI Systems are profound; they can severely affect the lives of Natural Persons by influencing crucial decisions related to risk assessment, eligibility for services, and even inferences drawn from biometric data. Such applications underscore the necessity for appropriate regulations to safeguard the rights and freedoms of Natural Persons, especially in high-risk scenarios where they may be subject to substantial influence from these AI-driven processes.&lt;SEP&gt;AI systems must not be used to profile natural persons for criminal activities, emphasizing the need for ethical standards.&lt;SEP&gt;The risk assessment of AI systems takes into account their potential impact on natural persons' health and rights.</data>
      <data key="d8">AI interaction,accessibility,biometric identification,classification,discrimination,ethical AI,ethical concerns,ethical considerations,evaluation,human engagement,human impact,impact,interaction,legal framework,legal implications,privacy issues,privacy rights,profiling,profiling prohibition,rights impact,rights protection,system design,transparency,user interaction,user rights,vulnerability</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-19b580305cd4ba71a7cf07559aa87a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42a8b91e450f13e8f10679d8814fac73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5eb0d79706d66f4b2474faeded28100a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62fe981c6c449c0068294efc1c020de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d36126d549d18d31512a9e98c8be8c3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bca7fe15bcbe076f97f98b2f3479775&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0c80a9013101dc32f29be5f179a89f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-593116201bc6ad18de913cc89dec1b6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b27dc39e557245814845f393e9bb76f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3200d57c186d7db38b0cfb2e5cddca1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f59c6f62bd0ab2d5521810dcbca8373d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbc4d8b4e0e145ee1cf76386d881919f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8871d96b04df0305284cbd20bfc2bf41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c3bb945919e1b0c6e146fabaaeff87b</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/132/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/5/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/6/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/59/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/42/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/32/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/15/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/70/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/31/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/93/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/44/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/58/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748428151</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AI Systems" target="Publicly Accessible Spaces">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">AI Systems are deployed in Publicly Accessible Spaces for real-time biometric identification under the provisions of the Regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;AI systems for biometric identification are used in publicly accessible spaces, enhancing law enforcement capabilities at the expense of personal freedom.</data>
      <data key="d8">public safety,surveillance,technology application,technology use</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b477112713d917a03ba6f595ed62f6c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62fe981c6c449c0068294efc1c020de6</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/32/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/38/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748428613</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AI Systems" target="Deployers">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Deployers are responsible for implementing and ensuring the proper use of AI systems according to defined instructions and obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;Deployers implement AI systems and must abide by regulations to inform users of their operations.</data>
      <data key="d8">implementation,oversight,regulation compliance,responsibility</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d466db10803ea1ed7cbdf3800e0caaee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-593116201bc6ad18de913cc89dec1b6b</data>
      <data key="d10">https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/recital/91/&lt;SEP&gt;https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/article/50/</data>
      <data key="d11">1748427864</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="AI Systems" target="High-Risk Activity">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">AI Systems that qualify as high-risk must adhere to specific regulations aimed at preventing misuse or harmful outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d8">regulatory compliance,risk management</data>
